 - **Screens whose rate is not a multiple of 60 (from PR #27).** A cheap 50 ms frame shows 54.5 ms at 165 Hz; counted as late it kept the game at 20 fps for good, so the frame step lets a frame on time show one refresh longer than its hold (`shown_slack`, `nvn_fstep.h`).
 - **Resolved: menu repeat twice as fast at 60 fps (2026-10-26).** `sead::ControllerBase::updateDerivativeParams_` (`main+0xeb0ef0`) adds a constant 1.0 (`main+0xeb0f78`..`88`) to each held button's count and repeats when `fmod(count - delay, pulse)` falls below that step, so delays and pulses were counted in frames: holding Down in the System menu for 0.3 s went 5 entries at 60 and 2 at 30 (found by MacNativeGames, `866beb9`). A `FRAME_STEP` stub at `main+0xeb0f88` takes the VFR's ratio (`+0x3c`, interval / 2) instead, keeping 1.0 when there is no VFR or the ratio is not positive. Mac, 60 fps: 0.2 / 0.3 / 0.4 s now move 1 / 2 / 3 entries, as the game does at 30. The pause menu's fast-repeat countdown (`main+0xe67d00`) already reads the VFR's delta.
 - `DM_NVN_CUT` growing segments (64, 128, 256 … draws) were tried for fewer commits: the game fell from 60 to 30 fps in the same scene (GPU idle while the long last segments were encoded).
+- **Above 60 fps** (`DM_FPS_TARGET=61`–`240` on a build whose rewriter table has `VAR_STEP`; not offered by the iOS launcher: not yet run on an iPhone): a variable frame step. Each frame the host publishes the time the previous one took as the VFR's frame ratio (in 30 fps frames, state page +0x50, which the `setWaitVsyncInterval` stub latches once a frame at +0x54 for the stub at `main+0x151d484`, so both VFR calls of a frame take one step), from which every per-core step is taken, so the game keeps real time at any frame rate; a scene that fixes its own interval (the VFR override) and prerendered movies keep the whole-vsync step. The display link runs at the cap (mode 4, `nvn_mtl.m` `link_range`; on a screen whose rate is not a multiple of it, virtual ticks at the cap) and each frame is shown on the next tick. Logic that stepped by the interval and not by the ratio ran at twice the speed at 120: the layout system's animation step (menus, title screen, HUD; `main+0x1280ec8`) and a fade (`main+0x125f114`) take max(interval, 1) × 0.5, and now the ratio; held buttons count the ratio through the menu-repeat stub above (`main+0xeb0f88`), so at the variable step too; the physics step runs round(6 × ratio) substeps (two at 120, where ragdolls blew apart) and gets at least 0.5, three. These sites come from MacNativeGames (PR #103) and were checked by disassembly. Steps have no floor at the cap: at 120 on a 144 Hz screen frames start 6.9 and 13.9 ms apart in turn, and a floor of 1/120 s ran the game at 1.12 of real time; the floor of 1/240 s carries what it adds into the next steps, and after a cutscene or movie the step starts again from the next frame ([Variable frame step above 60 fps](../../../docs/reference/frame-rate.md#variable-frame-step-above-60-fps)). On the Mac (M2 Max, 2560×1440): 119.5 fps at the title and in the menus at 1.000 of real time; in the open world about 84 fps at 1440p and 74 at 4K with the first implementation of the same step (PR #62), which ran two physics substeps a frame at 120 where this one runs three. Log: `variable frame step: … fps, game time … (… of real time)`.
 
